# Project: 1v1 Betting Telegram Bot with Chainlink Integration

## Project Overview
This is a Telegram bot that enables instant 1v1 cryptocurrency price betting between users in group chats. The system uses Chainlink Runtime Environment (CRE) for decentralized price feeds and automated settlement.

## Core Technology Stack
- **Blockchain**: EVM-compatible chains (Ethereum, Polygon, Arbitrum, etc.)
- **Chainlink Components**:
  - Data Feeds: Decentralized price oracles for cryptocurrency prices
  - Automation: Time-based automatic settlement execution
  - CRE (Chainlink Runtime Environment): Workflow orchestration and automation
- **Frontend**: Telegram Bot + WebApp (React, TailwindCSS, shadcn/ui)
- **Smart Contracts**: Solidity (Factory pattern for bet creation)
- **Backend**: Node.js/TypeScript for bot coordination
- **Wallet Integration**: WalletConnect v2

## Architecture Principles

### 1. Trust Model
- ALL financial logic MUST be on-chain and immutable
- Bot is ONLY a coordinator, never holds funds or executes settlements
- Price data MUST come from Chainlink Data Feeds only
- Settlement MUST be triggered by Chainlink Automation only
- No centralized backend can interfere with bet outcomes

### 2. Smart Contract Design
- Use Factory pattern for creating individual bet contracts
- Each bet is an isolated contract instance
- Bet parameters (amount, duration, asset) are immutable once created
- Settlement logic is deterministic: endPrice > startPrice = UP, endPrice < startPrice = DOWN, equal = REFUND
- Price precision must be consistent (use Chainlink Feed decimals)

### 3. Security Requirements
- Minimum bet duration: 3-5 minutes to reduce manipulation risk
- Implement reentrancy guards on all fund-handling functions
- Use SafeERC20 for token transfers
- Validate all Chainlink oracle responses (check staleness, heartbeat)
- Handle Automation delays gracefully with fallback mechanisms

### 4. User Experience Flow
- Users interact ONLY through Telegram (social layer)
- Financial transactions happen ONLY in wallet (trust layer)
- Bot updates a SINGLE message (no spam)
- All amounts and addresses are pre-filled (no manual input)
- Clear state transitions visible in group chat

## Chainlink CRE Integration Rules

### When Using CRE
- Use existing CRE knowledge as baseline for implementation
- Web-fetch latest documentation from chainlink-agent-skills/cre-skills/references/ only when:
  - Implementing specific new features with uncertain APIs
  - Encountering errors that may be due to API changes
  - Need to verify recent updates or deprecations
- Prefer TypeScript for CRE workflows unless explicitly requested otherwise

### CRE Workflow Patterns
- Use HTTP triggers for bot-initiated bet creation
- Use Cron/Time triggers for scheduled settlement checks
- Use EVM read capability to fetch Chainlink Data Feed prices
- Use EVM write capability to call settlement functions
- Store sensitive data (API keys, private keys) using CRE secrets management

### Price Feed Integration
- MUST use Chainlink Data Feeds for all price data
- Check feed heartbeat and deviation threshold
- Handle stale price data gracefully
- Support multiple networks (use appropriate feed addresses per network)

## Development Guidelines

### Smart Contract Development
- Use Foundry (forge, cast, anvil) for compilation, testing, and deployment
- Write comprehensive tests in Solidity using forge-std
- Test edge cases: equal prices, oracle failures, timeout scenarios
- Use OpenZeppelin contracts for standard implementations
- Document all functions with NatSpec comments
- Emit events for all state changes for off-chain tracking

### Development Tools
- **Foundry**: Smart contract development, testing, and deployment
- **CRE CLI**: Workflow management
- **The Graph** (optional): Event indexing
- **Tenderly**: Transaction simulation and debugging

### Telegram Bot Development
- Keep bot stateless (store state on-chain or in database)
- Use inline keyboards for all user interactions
- Update existing messages instead of sending new ones
- Handle concurrent user actions gracefully
- Provide clear error messages without exposing technical details

### WebApp Development
- Mobile-first responsive design
- Show real-time countdown timers
- Display Chainlink logo and "Powered by Chainlink" attribution
- Show transaction status clearly (pending, confirmed, failed)
- Allow users to return to incomplete bets

### Testing Strategy
- Unit tests for all smart contract functions
- Integration tests for Chainlink oracle interactions
- End-to-end tests for complete bet lifecycle
- Simulation tests using CRE local environment
- Test on testnet before mainnet deployment

## Code Style and Conventions

### Solidity
- Use Solidity 0.8.x with explicit version
- Follow Solidity style guide
- Use explicit visibility modifiers
- Prefer custom errors over require strings (gas optimization)
- Use events for all state changes

### TypeScript/JavaScript
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Follow ESLint rules
- Use async/await over promises
- Handle all errors explicitly
- Use environment variables for configuration

### Git Commit Messages
- Follow Conventional Commits specification
- Format: `type(scope): description`
- Types: feat, fix, chore, docs, test, refactor
- Keep descriptions concise and imperative mood

## File Organization (Monorepo)
Each module is an independent directory with its own config and env files.
Root directory contains ONLY: package.json (scripts to manage all modules), docs, and project-level config.
NO .env or module-specific config files in root.
```
/contracts          - Foundry project (smart contracts)
  /src              - Solidity source files
  /test             - Forge tests (Solidity)
  /script           - Forge deployment scripts
  /foundry.toml     - Foundry config
  /.env             - Contract-specific env vars
/cre-workflows      - Chainlink CRE workflows (own package.json)
/bot                - Telegram bot (own package.json, tsconfig, .env)
/webapp             - Telegram WebApp (own package.json, vite config, .env)
/docs               - Documentation
/package.json       - Root scripts only (no deps), orchestrates all modules
```

## Environment-Specific Rules

### Development
- Use testnet (Sepolia, Mumbai, etc.)
- Enable verbose logging
- Use mock oracles for rapid testing when appropriate
- Keep test data clearly marked

### Production
- NEVER hardcode private keys or API keys
- Use environment variables for all secrets
- Enable monitoring and alerting
- Set appropriate gas limits and prices
- Implement rate limiting on bot endpoints

## Critical Constraints

### Financial Safety
- Bet amounts must be locked before start price is recorded
- No partial withdrawals allowed
- Settlement can only happen once
- Failed settlements must allow manual intervention without breaking fairness

### Oracle Reliability
- Always check oracle response timestamp
- Implement circuit breakers for oracle failures
- Have fallback mechanisms for delayed Automation
- Log all oracle calls for audit trail

### User Protection
- Clear cancellation policy (before both parties commit)
- Automatic refund if counterparty doesn't join within timeout
- Transparent fee structure (if any)
- Clear dispute resolution process

